| 1 | 3 | Characters out of nothing. *How do actors invent a whole scene with no script, and how does a group make it better?* | 16 | th-g3-imagine; th-g3-show | TH:Cr1.1.3a; TH:Cr1.1.3c; TH:Cr2.1.3a; TH:Cr2.1.3b; TH:Cr3.1.3a; TH:Pr6.1.3a | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3); performing (Pr6). Works: commedia dell'arte, the improvised comedy of Italy in the 1500s and 1600s, from the scenarios Flaminio Scala printed in Venice in 1611 (Il teatro delle favole rappresentative, public domain): one-page plot outlines posted backstage that the players filled with their own words, described and cut by Curio Class; Viola Spolin (United States, 1906 to 1994), whose theater games (the "Where" game: a place shown by the objects you use in it) are described in our own words and credited to her | Retrieval in day 2's opener, with its reteach: a "what if" detail and a made-up line (th-g2-imagine), the group agreements (th-g2-show). TH:Cr1.1.3a: make up characters, an imagined place and an improvised story, from a scenario card. TH:Cr1.1.3c: decide as a group how each character moves and speaks to serve the story. TH:Cr2.1.3a: try a method of finding ideas of the student's own (the what-if list, the "Where" game, an object passed round and used three ways). TH:Cr2.1.3b: compare ideas with the group and keep the ones that make the scene stronger. TH:Cr3.1.3a: rework the scene with the group so it fits a set limit (two minutes on the teacher's timer, a pace cue, or a space marked on the floor). TH:Pr6.1.3a: rehearse the scene, then reflect alone and with the group on the rehearsal-log page. Technique: the scenario-to-scene steps (read the outline, cast, find the place, run, freeze, fix) and the freeze. Words: improvise, setting, character, method, group, idea, rehearse, limit, reflect, strengthen. Showing: each group's two-minute scenario scene for the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 14, written day 15), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: a character and a place the group invented, clear to the audience; TH:Cr1.1.3c, a guide look-for on the showing: the agreed way the student's character moves and speaks; TH:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene fits the two-minute limit and the marked space; TH:Pr6.1.3a, the kept rehearsal-log page from the last rehearsal day, one line alone and one with the group; TH:Cr2.1.3a, the kept idea sheet: the method the student tried and the idea it gave; TH:Cr2.1.3b, a written item: from two ideas for the unseen scene, the one the student would keep and why it makes the scene stronger. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: given circumstances (grade 4), staging problems (grade 6). Tools: scenario cards, step cards, the idea sheet, the rehearsal-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, a scarf, the voice. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 3 because every later unit improvises and rehearses in groups: the rehearsal-log model starts here and the what-if list and group agreements are retrieved where they are first needed this year. Commedia's posted outline makes improvising inside a limit concrete before the actor's tools are built in unit 2 | Candidate: a third improvising tradition from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Commedia used half-masks and stock types, some tied to regions: students play a character's want and walk, never an accent, a regional type or a mask of a real group; masks are studied in pictures only. Hazards and controls: a clear floor, chairs to the walls; freezes held still, no falls; the passed object checked by the teacher. A student who does not want to perform takes the timer job or the "Where" objects and is marked on the idea sheet and log. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 3 | The actor's tools. *How do actors use body, voice and mind to make a character, and why did they choose what they did?* | 16 | th-g3-actor; th-g3-notice | TH:Pr4.1.3b; TH:Pr5.1.3a; TH:Cr3.1.3b; TH:Re7.1.3a; TH:Re8.1.3a; TH:Re8.1.3c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: kyogen, the comic theater of Japan played between noh plays (on UNESCO's list of the intangible heritage of humanity with noh as nogaku theater since 2008), whose actors use a set walk, a big clear voice and pretend actions with a fan, studied from UNESCO's page and a Japanese cultural body's page, and performed today by family lines of players; Bill Irwin (United States, born 1950; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a clown and actor who builds characters from the body, described and linked to a public page on his work | Retrieval in day 2's opener, with its reteach: one character walked and spoken two ways (th-g2-actor), an audience card on a decision moment (th-g2-notice). TH:Pr5.1.3a: take part in sets of body, voice and thinking exercises for group work (a body set: shake out, levels, a walk; a voice set: hum, siren, a line to the back wall; a thinking set: a memory game, the count-to-twenty). TH:Pr4.1.3b: look at how a drama uses movement and voice (the kyogen walk and voice, Irwin's body). TH:Cr3.1.3b: explore body and voice for a character in an unscripted and a scripted scene (a two-line Curio Class script). TH:Re7.1.3a: understand the reasons behind artists' decisions (why a kyogen actor shows a door with a fan). TH:Re8.1.3a: name several of your own experiences of joining in or watching drama. TH:Re8.1.3c: say how you connect with a character's feelings. Technique: the character-map (moves, speaks, thinks) and the three exercise sets. Words: exercise, movement, voice, body, explore, character, reason, decision, experience, connect. Showing: a two-line scene played twice, once unscripted and once from the script, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 30, written day 31), id by id: TH:Cr3.1.3b, a guide look-for on the showing: body and voice explored for the character, the two versions different; TH:Pr5.1.3a, a guide look-for on the warm-up that opens the showing day: the student joins each of the three sets; TH:Pr4.1.3b, the kept character-map from the last rehearsal day: the movement and voice the student chose and where a work showed it; TH:Re7.1.3a, a kept audience-journal card from the showing day: one choice another group made and the reason behind it; TH:Re8.1.3c, a written item on the unseen scene: a character's feeling and how the student connects with it; TH:Re8.1.3a, a written item: two of the student's experiences of joining in or watching drama this year (a class scene, a show, a story told at home, or an invented one, marked the same). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a chosen exercise practiced as a technique (grade 4). Tools: exercise-set cards, the character-map, audience cards, the two-line script, Curio Class's own. Free path: the floor and the voice. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because the class can already improvise in groups and now needs tools for the characters it invents; before the mid-year check (week 17) and the design unit, whose costume pieces show characters the students can already move and speak. The warm-up-routine and audience-journal are retrieved here and the character-map starts | Candidate: an exercise set drawn from a second tradition (for example a Brazilian or Indian theater game), from the tradition's own published teaching | Kyogen: students study the walk and voice from pictures and descriptions and use their own characters, never a kyogen costume, mask or a Japanese voice; the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Japanese cultural body's page first, and the unit plan picks a play free of drinking and harm. Experiences of watching drama: no student needs a paid show; a class scene or an invented experience counts the same. Hazards and controls: arms-out space; voice sets kept below shouting, a rest after the siren; no spinning past three turns. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 3 | Dressing the story. *How do costumes, props and sets fit a story's world, and how does a drama show the people who make it?* | 18 | th-g3-design; th-g3-world | TH:Cr1.1.3b; TH:Pr5.1.3b; TH:Cr3.1.3c; TH:Re9.1.3b; TH:Cn10.1.3a; TH:Cn11.1.3a; TH:Re8.1.3b | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re8, Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: L. Frank Baum's The Wonderful Wizard of Oz (United States, 1900, public domain), with W. W. Denslow's first illustrations, beside The Wiz (Broadway, 1975), the stage retelling with an all-Black cast whose costumes were designed by Geoffrey Holder (born in Trinidad, 1930 to 2014), described from a museum's or a public archive's page and never copied: one story, two designs; Roadside Theater (Whitesburg, Kentucky, founded 1975), an Appalachian company that makes plays from its own community's stories, described and linked to its own pages | Retrieval in day 2's opener, with its reteach: a prop-sketch of one place (th-g2-design), the two-ties card (th-g2-world). TH:Cr1.1.3b: imagine and describe costume, prop and set ideas that suit a story's setting and characters, on the prop-sketch with labels. TH:Pr5.1.3b: name the basic tech elements a drama can use (costume, prop, set, light, sound, makeup named but not used). TH:Cr3.1.3c: rehearse with the costume piece and prop and polish one design choice. TH:Re9.1.3b: weigh and analyze the tech elements across several works (Denslow's pictures, Holder's costumes as described, and a class scene). TH:Cn10.1.3a: make a short piece that draws on what the student knows and has lived, to connect with a community (the school's, the town's, or an invented town's, marked the same). TH:Cn11.1.3a: spot where the drama ties to community life, a social problem or another subject (the two-ties card). TH:Re8.1.3b: weigh ways to build a character with looks, props and costume pieces that show a cultural view, checked against a source (Holder's choices in his own or a museum's words, never a costume of a real group made by the class). Technique: the labeled design sketch, a costume piece from one fabric scrap and tape, a set model from a folded card. Words: set, costume, prop, tech element, design, decision, community, culture, social problem, subject, view. Showing: each group's short community scene in its designed costume pieces and set, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 50, written day 51), id by id: TH:Cn10.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene draws on a community the students know (or the invented town); TH:Cr3.1.3c, a guide look-for on the showing: the polished design choice used in the run; TH:Cr1.1.3b, the kept prop-sketch: costume, prop and set ideas labeled, with one line on why they suit the setting and the characters; TH:Cn11.1.3a, the kept two-ties card: one community tie and one tie to a social problem or another subject; TH:Pr5.1.3b, a written item: three tech elements the unseen scene could use, named; TH:Re9.1.3b, a written item: one tech element compared across two of the unit's works; TH:Re8.1.3b, the prop-sketch's character box, scored with it: a look, a prop and a costume piece chosen for the student's character and the source line that checks the cultural view. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: lights and sound serving a central idea (grade 4). Tools: the design sketch sheet, the two-ties card, work cards, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: scrap fabric, old shirts, cereal-box card, chairs as a set. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 2 because a costume piece shows a character the students can already move and speak, and after unit 1's group rehearsal. Longest year 3 unit: seven ids and two products, the first design sketches that must suit a setting and the first community and culture work checked against a source. The prop-sketch and two-ties models are retrieved here, where first needed this year | Candidate: a design by a maker from Asia or Africa compared beside the two Oz designs | Community: the student's own community is the starting point and no student speaks for a whole group; an invented town is offered beside it and marked the same. Social problems are ones a child can name in their own school or town (litter, a crowded bus, a closed playground), never a student's family trouble. The Wiz: the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or public archive's page on Holder's designs and his own published words first; no student dresses as a real people. Roadside Theater: its own pages read first. Hazards and controls: blunt scissors; tape, no pins; costume pieces loose and quick to remove, nothing tied round the neck; set models of card on the floor, never stood on. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 3 | From page to stage. *How does a written story become a play, and how does an audience decide what it thinks?* | 10 | th-g3-story; th-g3-judge | TH:Pr4.1.3a; TH:Cn11.2.3a; TH:Cn11.2.3b; TH:Re9.1.3a; TH:Re9.1.3c | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: The Bremen Town Musicians, from the Grimm brothers' tales (Germany, in their collection from the 1819 edition, public domain), retold by Curio Class, the written story the class turns into a play; the same tale retold in bronze by Gerhard Marcks's statue in Bremen (1953), described and linked; the griots (jeli) of the Mande peoples of West Africa, who retell long stories from memory with music and change each telling for its audience, and the Sosso-Bala, the balafon kept by one griot family in Guinea (on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2008), studied from UNESCO's page and listened to only | Retrieval in day 2's opener, with its reteach: a story map with a "because" arrow (th-g2-story), a character's problem from the audience's seat (th-g2-notice). TH:Cn11.2.3a: look at how a written story is reshaped into a stage piece (what is cut, what becomes action, what becomes a line). TH:Pr4.1.3a: use the parts of dramatic structure (a beginning, a problem, a climb, an end) on the story to build the class's play, on the story map. TH:Cn11.2.3b: examine how artists of the past retold one story in different art forms and ways (the Grimms' book, a statue, a griot's retelling for a new audience). TH:Re9.1.3a: understand why and how groups judge a drama (an audience at the end, a class, a festival panel), and make the class criteria card. TH:Re9.1.3c: judge the problems and situations the play's characters face as an audience member would (the robbers' house, the animals' plan). Technique: cutting a tale into three scenes and a freeze-frame climax; the criteria card. Words: structure, beginning, middle, end, convention, retell, adapt, judge, audience, situation. Showing: the class play of The Bremen Town Musicians, in groups of five each playing the whole short play, then shown to families on day 64. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 61, written day 62), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: the group's play has a clear beginning, problem and end; TH:Cn11.2.3a, the kept story map from the last rehearsal day: one part of the written story and what it became on stage; TH:Re9.1.3a, the kept criteria card: two things the class audience looks for and why groups judge a play; TH:Cn11.2.3b, a written item: one way a past artist retold the tale differently from the Grimms' book; TH:Re9.1.3c, a written item on the unseen scene: the problem a character faces, judged from the audience's seat with a reason. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Day 64, after the respond day, holds the family showing. Out: rewriting an outcome (grade 4), a judging guide the student makes (grade 5). Tools: the story map, the retelling, the criteria card, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the year's costume pieces and the voice. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in year 3 because the play uses the year's improvising, actor's tools and designs (units 1 to 3) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 27); the story-map is retrieved here and the criteria-card starts. Judging joins the story unit because the class first judges a play it has made and watched whole. Short because the tale is short and its showing uses the year's props and warm-ups | Candidate: a griot practitioner's own published words on retelling, found and credited by the unit plan | The robbers are chased off by noise and a scare, played by the teacher or by chairs, never by a student; no student is hurt or plays being hurt. Griots and the Sosso-Bala: studied by hearing and looking only, from UNESCO's page and the griots' own published words, read first by the sensitivity agent; no student imitates a griot's music or titles, and the balafon's sacred keeping is named plainly (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). Judging is of the work, never of the maker. Family yes for showing outside class; a recorded or solo version is offered. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 4 | Who, where, when. *How do the given circumstances change every idea, and what jobs does a show need?* | 16 | th-g4-imagine; th-g4-show | TH:Cr1.1.4a; TH:Cr1.1.4c; TH:Cr2.1.4a; TH:Cr2.1.4b; TH:Cr3.1.4a; TH:Pr6.1.4a | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3); performing (Pr6). Works: Konstantin Stanislavski (Russia, 1863 to 1938), who named the actor's "given circumstances" (who, where, when and what has happened), described in our own words from a public domain source the unit plan records; wayang kulit, the shadow puppet theater of Indonesia (on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2008), in which one puppeteer, the dalang, voices every character, moves every puppet and leads the musicians, studied from UNESCO's page and an Indonesian cultural body's page: one person doing the jobs a class shares | Retrieval in day 2's opener: inventing by improvising (th-g3-imagine), rehearse and reflect (th-g3-show). TH:Cr1.1.4a: describe how an imagined world looks and how an improvised story fits its given circumstances. TH:Cr1.1.4c: picture how a character moves to fit them (a walk at night, in a storm, late for school). TH:Cr2.1.4a: question the characters and the plot together to invent ideas (hot seat: one student in role, the group asks). TH:Cr2.1.4b: decide together and list the jobs a showing needs (director, stage manager, actors, sound, props, house manager). TH:Cr3.1.4a: revise the scene by repeating it and reviewing it together, with the first critique the students run (the critique-protocol card). TH:Pr6.1.4a: show the small group's work to classmates as the audience. Technique: the given-circumstances card and the hot seat; the run, review, rerun cycle. Words: given circumstances, world, question, improvise, invent, plot, job, revise, review, audience. Showing: each group's given-circumstances scene shown to classmates, run by its own stage manager. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 78, written day 79), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.4c, a guide look-for on the showing: the student's character moves to fit who, where and when; TH:Pr6.1.4a, a guide look-for on the showing: the group's work shown to the class as audience, each student in their place; TH:Cr3.1.4a, the kept rehearsal-log page: what changed between the first and the second run and which critique point caused it; TH:Cr2.1.4b, the kept jobs list made by the group, with the student's job and what it does; TH:Cr2.1.4a, the kept hot-seat sheet: two questions the student asked a character and the idea one gave; TH:Cr1.1.4a, a written item on the unseen scene: how its world looks and how the story fits its given circumstances. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: ideas from a character's inner life (grade 5). Tools: given-circumstances cards, the critique-protocol card, jobs cards, the rehearsal-log, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, a scarf, the voice. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 4 because the given-circumstances model steers every later unit this year (unit 7's designs serve it), and named jobs and the student-run critique are needed before the year's longer rehearsals. Wayang's one-person show makes the list of jobs concrete | Candidate: a Stanislavski exercise checked against a public domain English source for its wording | Wayang tells stories from the Mahabharata and Ramayana and is played at ceremonies: it is studied by looking only, from UNESCO's page and an Indonesian cultural body's page read first by the sensitivity agent; no student makes or moves a wayang figure or imitates the dalang's voice. Hot seat: the student in role answers as an invented character, never as themself, and may pass. Hazards and controls: a clear floor; the director's job never includes touching actors; stage managers call "freeze" for safety. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 4 | Walks and faces. *How does a character's walk tell us who they are, and what do feelings do to the body?* | 16 | th-g4-actor; th-g4-notice | TH:Pr4.1.4b; TH:Pr5.1.4a; TH:Cr3.1.4b; TH:Re7.1.4a; TH:Re8.1.4a; TH:Re8.1.4c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: Charlie Chaplin (born in England, 1889 to 1977), the Tramp's walk and posture in The Gold Rush (United States, its 1925 release, public domain in the United States), shown from a public archive's copy or described; Deaf West Theatre (Los Angeles, founded 1991), whose plays, such as Big River (Broadway, 2003), are performed in American Sign Language and spoken English at once, so meaning lives in the body and the hands, described and linked to its own pages | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the three exercise sets (th-g3-actor), reasons behind artists' decisions (th-g3-notice). TH:Pr4.1.4b: choose how the body moves to build a character (the walk led by the nose, the knees, the chest; posture high and low). TH:Pr5.1.4a: practice a chosen ensemble exercise (the mirror, the group walk, the machine) as a technique. TH:Cr3.1.4b: build two body and voice techniques for a scripted or unscripted scene and name them. TH:Re7.1.4a: spot the choices artists made in a piece by joining in and watching (the Tramp's walk, a Deaf West actor's sign and body). TH:Re8.1.4a: compare and contrast experiences of joining in or watching with a partner. TH:Re8.1.4c: spot and talk about how a feeling changes the body (look, describe, interpret on a clip or a live scene). Technique: the three-centers walk, the mirror exercise, look-describe-interpret. Words: technique, exercise, practice, choose, posture, character, choice, compare, contrast, feeling, body. Showing: a wordless scene of two characters with different walks, then the same scene with lines, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 94, written day 95), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.4b, a guide look-for on the showing: the chosen walk and posture build the character; TH:Pr5.1.4a, a guide look-for on the warm-up that opens the showing day: the student's chosen ensemble exercise practiced with the group; TH:Cr3.1.4b, the kept character-map: two body and voice techniques the student used, named; TH:Re7.1.4a, a kept audience-journal card from the showing day: one choice another group's actor made; TH:Re8.1.4a, the kept partner card from the last rehearsal day: one experience alike and one different, written with a partner; TH:Re8.1.4c, a written item on the unseen scene (performed by the teacher): how a feeling changed the character's body. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: physical actions that carry meaning (grade 5). Tools: walk cards, the character-map, audience cards, a clip viewer or the printed stills, Curio Class's own. Free path: the floor and the voice. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because a character's walk now fits given circumstances the class can name; before the mid-year check (week 17) and unit 7, whose costumes and lights serve characters the students can already embody. The look-describe-interpret model starts here, where reading the body first needs it | Candidate: a third physical-theater maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Chaplin's Tramp is a poor man: the unit studies the walk and the comedy, never poverty as a joke; no student plays a disability by its walk. Deaf West: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own pages and Deaf community sources first; no student imitates signing as a gimmick, and a Deaf or hard-of-hearing student's own language is welcome in every scene. Hazards and controls: the mirror exercise keeps an arm's length, no touching; the machine exercise stays on the floor, no climbing on a partner. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 4 | Light, sound and point of view. *How do lights and sound serve a story, and whose view does a play show?* | 16 | th-g4-design; th-g4-world | TH:Cr1.1.4b; TH:Pr5.1.4b; TH:Cr3.1.4c; TH:Re9.1.4b; TH:Cn10.1.4a; TH:Cn11.1.4a; TH:Re8.1.4b | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re8, Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Adolphe Appia (Switzerland, 1862 to 1928), whose drawings of stages built from steps and shadow showed light as the main tool of a design, public domain, from a museum's or library's open page; Larissa FastHorse (a citizen of the Sicangu Lakota Nation, born 1971; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a playwright who rewrote the Native characters and scenes of J. M. Barrie's Peter Pan (1904) for a touring production in 2023 and 2024 so a Native family is shown as its own people tell it, studied from her own published words and the production's pages, described, never copied; wayang's single lamp from unit 5, revisited: one light making a whole world | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the labeled design sketch (th-g3-design), the two-ties card (th-g3-world). TH:Cr1.1.4b: picture and plan a set, lights and sound that serve the story's given circumstances. TH:Re9.1.4b: look into how tech can back up a drama's central idea (Appia's light, the class's scenes). TH:Pr5.1.4b: suggest a way to use a tech part in the class play (a flashlight sunrise, a sound made by the group). TH:Cr3.1.4c: as a group, fix a design or tech snag found in rehearsal (a light too late, a set piece in the way). TH:Cn10.1.4a: name ways a play shows a community's or culture's view (FastHorse's rewrite, told in her words). TH:Cn11.1.4a: react to a local problem in a scene that uses facts from another subject (a fact card on the school's water use or its playground's litter, or an invented town's problem). TH:Re8.1.4b: compare characters' qualities through looks, props and costume pieces that show a cultural view, drawn from a source (the class's own invented characters, each choice backed by its card). Technique: the light plan (where the light comes from, what it shows) and a sound made on cue. Words: lighting, sound, set, snag, central idea, given circumstances, perspective, local, problem, compare, culture. Showing: each group's local-problem scene with one light and one sound cue, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 112, written day 113), id by id: TH:Cn11.1.4a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene reacts to a local problem and uses a fact from the fact card; TH:Pr5.1.4b, a guide look-for on the showing: the student's suggested tech part used at its cue; TH:Cr1.1.4b, the kept prop-sketch: the set, light and sound plan with one line on how each serves the given circumstances; TH:Cr3.1.4c, the kept rehearsal-log page: the snag, the group's fix and whether it worked; TH:Re8.1.4b, the kept character card: two characters' looks, props and costume pieces compared, each with its source line; TH:Re9.1.4b, a written item: how one tech element in the unseen scene or a unit work backs up its central idea; TH:Cn10.1.4a, a written item: one way a play studied in the unit shows a community's view. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a cue sheet and tech that carries a theme (grade 5). Tools: the design sketch sheet, the light plan, fact cards, the two-ties card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the room lights switched by the teacher, a window blind, body and found-object sounds. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 6 because lights and costumes serve characters the students can already walk and speak, and after unit 5's given circumstances, which the designs must serve. Before unit 8 because judging how a character's choice changes what an audience sees uses the year's lit scenes | Candidate: a lighting or sound designer from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Peter Pan: the 1904 play's Native characters were a stereotype; the unit tells this in one plain sentence from FastHorse's own published words, shows no picture of the old costumes, and no student plays or dresses as a Native character; the sensitivity agent reads FastHorse's words and a Sicangu Lakota or Native-run source first. Local problems: small and public (water, litter, traffic), never a student's home; an invented town is offered beside it and marked the same. Hazards and controls: LED flashlights with screw-closed battery doors, no coin or button batteries, never shone into eyes, no flashing; the room dimmed, never dark, with the door light on; sound effects kept below shouting. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
