ch, cue, express, problem, fix, link, community, worldwide, view, response. Showing: each group's scene on a problem, run from its cue sheet, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 178, written day 179), id by id: TH:Pr5.1.5b, a guide look-for on the showing: the student runs or shows the cue as the cue sheet says; TH:Cn11.1.5a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene gives a voice to a past, worldwide or social problem from its fact card; TH:Cr1.1.5b, the kept prop-sketch: design ideas, each with one line on the given circumstances it serves; TH:Cr3.1.5c, the kept rehearsal-log page: the problem and the fix the student invented; TH:Re9.1.5b, a written item: how one tech element in a unit work or the unseen scene expresses its theme; TH:Cn10.1.5a, a written item: how one of the unit's theaters linked people with their community; TH:Re8.1.5b, a written item on the unseen scene: how a cultural view, the student's own or an invented viewer's, shapes a response to a character. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: production elements weighed in a show's design (grade 6). Tools: the cue sheet, fact cards, the design sketch sheet, the two-ties card, Curio Class's own. Free path: card signs, the room lights, body and found-object sounds. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 10 because a sign or a cue only carries a theme for characters the students can already embody, and after unit 7's light and sound. Before unit 12 because judging a play by a guide uses the year's scenes and cues. The cue-sheet model starts here | Candidate: a community theater from Asia, described and linked | The grape strike and Kamiriithu are hard history: Kamiriithu's play was stopped by the government and Ng�cg�c wa Thiong'o was held in prison without trial in 1978; the unit says each in one plain sentence from the theaters' own or a museum's or university's published words, read first by the sensitivity agent. No student plays a farmworker, a boss, a prisoner or