layed with its hidden thought readable in the acting; TH:Cn11.2.5b, the kept script page from the last rehearsal day: two stage terms from the old source, each explained and used; TH:Re9.1.5a, the kept judging guide made by the class, with the student's marks on another group's play; TH:Cn11.2.5a, a written item: one trait the three tellings share and one that belongs to one culture's telling; TH:Re9.1.5c, a written item on the unseen scene: how a character's situation changes how the student, as audience, views the piece. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Day 192, after the respond day, holds the family showing. Out: key events and two versions of one stage story (grade 6), judgment with evidence and criteria (grade 6). Tools: the story map, the three retellings, the judging guide, the First Folio page from a library's open copy or its printed copy, Curio Class's own. Free path: the year's costume pieces and the voice. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in the band because comparing versions before making a play and judging it with the class's own guide use every thread of the year (units 9 to 11) and end the band after the year-end check (week 27); the story-map and criteria-card come back for their last step here | Candidate: a fourth telling from Africa or Southeast Asia, from a public domain source | The Poor Turkey Girl: a Zuni story recorded by an outsider in 1901; the sensitivity agent reads the Zuni Pueblo's own museum and heritage pages (the A:shiwi A:wan Museum and Heritage Center) first, the unit uses the tale only if those sources treat it as open to be told, and otherwise swaps it for another culture's telling with the ids kept; it is studied by reading and comparing, never acted as a Zuni character, costume or dance. Stepfamily cruelty in Cinderella stories: told plainly, played by an object or the teacher, never by a student against a student; no student is asked about their own family. Family yes for showing outside class; a recorded or solo version is offered. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

