Each check day: set-up 3, warm-up 5, six groups of five play the fresh scene for about 4 minutes each (24) while the
others are the audience and fill the card the check uses, close 4; the teacher marks one group at a time and reads
the cards after the session. Every student's evidence is collected once in a window; no artifact is scored twice.
Days above are band course days (year 4 starts on day 65, year 5 on day 129).

## Cross-subject

Three theater rows of grades 3 to 5 carry a cross_subject lean on English language arts (data/progressions/arts.csv):
ela:RL.3.3 (th-g3-story), ela:SL.4.4 (th-g4-actor) and ela:RL.5.3 (th-g5-story). All are core courses (lesson 32);
each is practiced here, not owned, and the theater unit does not reteach it (STANDARD.md 6).

### English language arts, grade 3 (same year)

| Used here | Lean | Where ELA teaches it | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 4, days 55 to 64 (weeks 28 to 32 of year 3): how a character's actions move the story, in the play made from the tale (th-g3-story) | RL.3.3 | ELA grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39) | Taught by then. If a school runs ELA late, the story map's "because" arrows carry the characters' actions and the teacher models one before the students try |

### English language arts, grade 4 (same year)

| Used here | Lean | Where ELA teaches it | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 6, band days 81 to 96 (days 17 to 32 of year 4, weeks 9 to 16): speaking clearly in a character's voice at a pace the audience can follow (th-g4-actor) | SL.4.4 | ELA grade 4 unit 1 (days 1 to 38) | Taught by then. If not, the theater unit marks only the character's body and voice choices, never a whole spoken report, and the warm-up card shows "to the back wall, one word at a time" |

### English language arts, grade 5 (same year)

| Used here | Lean | Where ELA teaches it | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 12, band days 183 to 192 (days 55 to 64 of year 5, weeks 28 to 32): comparing characters and events across three tellings before the play is made (th-g5-story) | RL.5.3 | ELA grade 5 unit 1 (days 1 to 41) | Taught by then. If not, the comparison chart gives the three tellings' characters and events in rows, and the teacher models one comparison first |

Written audience-journal entries and defended responses (units 2, 6, 10) practice opinion writing with a reason
(W.3.1, W.4.1, W.5.1): practiced, not owned. ELA teaches them in grade 3 unit 3 (days 81 to 119), grade 4 unit 3
(days 81 to 119) and grade 5 unit 3 (days 84 to 123). If ELA has not taught them yet, the response card gives the
frame ("I think ___ because ___. I saw it when ___.") and at most two written items a student. Talk about scenes and
the critique practice collaborative conversation (SL.3.1, SL.4.1, SL.5.1): practiced, not owned; the group agreements
and critique-protocol cards show the turn-taking steps.

### Science, social studies and mathematics, grades 3 to 5

No lean either way. Unit 7's light and sound cues and unit 11's cues sit near grade 4 science's light and sound
work; the theater units name light and sound only by their use in a scene and ask no science explanation. Units 3, 7
and 11 take their community and problem facts from fact cards Curio Class writes, and units 8, 11 and 12 teach the
setting of each work themselves, on work cards (bar criterion 7). No unit asks for number work (lesson 13): the
two-minute limit of unit 1 is kept on the teacher's timer as a pace cue, and no student measures or counts time.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks.** This frame's checks stay off every core check week: mid-year weeks 17 (years 3 and 4)
   and 18 (year 5); year-end week 27 (years 3 and 5) and 26 (year 4). Chosen by unit load, not to dodge other
   after-core courses; any sharing with other after-core courses in `frame.py calendar` is left for the lead's one
   calendar pass.
2. **Once-a-week respond sessions.** On the once-a-week path the year-end respond session falls in week 28 in year 3
   and week 27 in year 4, which hold social studies year-end checks in grades 3 and 4; they are respond sessions, not
