286: | 6 | 4 | Walks and faces. *How does a character's walk tell us who they are, and what do feelings do to the body?* | 16 | th-g4-actor; th-g4-notice | TH:Pr4.1.4b; TH:Pr5.1.4a; TH:Cr3.1.4b; TH:Re7.1.4a; TH:Re8.1.4a; TH:Re8.1.4c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: Charlie Chaplin (born in England, 1889 to 1977), the Tramp's walk and posture in The Gold Rush (United States, its 1925 release, public domain in the United States), shown from a public archive's copy or described; Deaf West Theatre (Los Angeles, founded 1991), whose plays, such as Big River (Broadway, 2003), are performed in American Sign Language and spoken English at once, so meaning lives in the body and the hands, described and linked to its own pages | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the three exercise sets (th-g3-actor), reasons behind artists' decisions (th-g3-notice). TH:Pr4.1.4b: choose how the body moves to build a character (the walk led by the nose, the knees, the chest; posture high and low). TH:Pr5.1.4a: practice a chosen ensemble exercise (the mirror, the group walk, the machine) as a technique. TH:Cr3.1.4b: build two body and voice techniques for a scripted or unscripted scene and name them. TH:Re7.1.4a: spot the choices artists made in a piece by joining in and watching (the Tramp's walk, a Deaf West actor's sign and body). TH:Re8.1.4a: compare and contrast experiences of joining in or watching with a partner. TH:Re8.1.4c: spot and talk about how a feeling changes the body (look, describe, interpret on a clip or a live scene). Technique: the three-centers walk, the mirror exercise, look-describe-interpret. Words: technique, exercise, practice, choose, posture, character, choice, compare, contrast, feeling, body. Showing: a wordless scene of two characters with different walks, then the same scene with lines, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 94, written day 95), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.4b, a guide look-for on the showing: the chosen walk and posture build the character; TH:Pr5.1.4a, a guide look-for on the warm-up that opens the showing day: the student's chosen ensemble exercise practiced with the group; TH:Cr3.1.4b, the kept character-map: two body and voice techniques the student used, named; TH:Re7.1.4a, a kept audience-journal card from the showing day: one choice another group's actor made; TH:Re8.1.4a, the kept partner card from the last rehearsal day: one experience alike and one different, written with a partner; TH:Re8.1.4c, a written item on the unseen scene (performed by the teacher): how a feeling changed the character's body. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: physical actions that carry meaning (grade 5). Tools: walk cards, the character-map, audience cards, a clip viewer or the printed stills, Curio Class's own. Free path: the floor and the voice. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because a character's walk now fits given circumstances the class can name; before the mid-year check (week 17) and unit 7, whose costumes and lights serve characters the students can already embody. The look-describe-interpret model starts here, where reading the body first needs it | Candidate: a third physical-theater maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Chaplin's Tramp is a poor man: the unit studies the walk and the comedy, never poverty as a joke; no student plays a disability by its walk. Deaf West: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own pages and Deaf community sources first; no student imitates signing as a gimmick, and a Deaf or hard-of-hearing student's own language is welcome in every scene. Hazards and controls: the mirror exercise keeps an arm's length, no touching; the machine exercise stays on the floor, no climbing on a partner. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
