invented characters, each choice backed by its card). Technique: the light plan (where the light comes from, what it shows) and a sound made on cue. Words: lighting, sound, set, snag, central idea, given circumstances, perspective, local, problem, compare, culture. Showing: each group's local-problem scene with one light and one sound cue, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 112, written day 113), id by id: TH:Cn11.1.4a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene reacts to a local problem and uses a fact from the fact card; TH:Pr5.1.4b, a guide look-for on the showing: the student's suggested tech part used at its cue; TH:Cr1.1.4b, the kept prop-sketch: the set, light and sound plan with one line on how each serves the given circumstances; TH:Cr3.1.4c, the kept rehearsal-log page: the snag, the group's fix and whether it worked; TH:Re8.1.4b, the kept character card: two characters' looks, props and costume pieces compared, each with its source line; TH:Re9.1.4b, a written item: how one tech element in the unseen scene or a unit work backs up its central idea; TH:Cn10.1.4a, a written item: one way a play studied in the unit shows a community's view. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a cue sheet and tech that carries a theme (grade 5). Tools: the design sketch sheet, the light plan, fact cards, the two-ties card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the room lights switched by the teacher, a window blind, body and found-object sounds. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 6 because lights and costumes serve characters the students can already walk and speak, and after unit 5's given circumstances, which the designs must serve. Before unit 8 because judging how a character's choice changes what an audience sees uses the year's lit scenes | Candidate: a lighting or sound designer from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Peter Pan: the 1904 play's Native characters were a stereotype; the unit tells this in one plain sentence from FastHorse's own published words, shows no picture of the old costumes, and no student plays or dresses as a Native character; the sensitivity agent reads FastHorse's words and a Sicangu Lakota or Native-run source first. Local problems: small and public (water, litter, traffic), never a student's home; an invented town is offered beside it and marked the same. Hazards and controls: LED flashlights with screw-closed battery doors, no coin or button batteries, never shone into eyes, no flashing; the room dimmed, never dark, with the door light on; sound effects kept below shouting. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

