roup's scene played with its old and its new ending, for families on day 128. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 125, written day 126), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.4a, a guide look-for on the showing: the rewritten lines and actions change the ending; TH:Re9.1.4c, a kept audience-journal card from the showing day: how one character's choice changed what the student saw in another group's two endings; TH:Cn11.2.4b, the kept era chart from the last rehearsal day: two stage practices of Epidaurus set against today's; TH:Re9.1.4a, the kept criteria card: the student's own offered way of judging, with a reason; TH:Cn11.2.4a, a written item: one way the meddah's and Mhlophe's storytelling differ and one way they are alike. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Day 128, after the respond day, holds the family showing. Out: hidden motives and versions compared before writing (grade 5). Tools: the story map, the era chart, the criteria card, the retelling, Curio Class's own. Free path: the year's costume pieces and a handkerchief. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in year 4 because rewriting an ending uses the year's given circumstances, walks and lit scenes (units 5 to 7) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26). Two days longer than year 3's story unit: it compares two cultures and an era besides the rewrite | Candidate: a third storyteller from the Americas, described and linked | Greek drama was made for festivals of the god Dionysus: named plainly, studied by looking at the theater and masks only, no ritual imitated, and students make no mask of a god. Meddah and Mhlophe: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and Mhlophe's own published words first; no student copies a meddah's dress or an accent. Nasreddin Hodja is a shared figure in several countries: the unit plan picks a tale free of mockery of any group. Family yes for showing outside class; a recorded or solo version is offered. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
289: | 9 | 5 | Inside the character. *How does what a character thinks inside change what they do, and how does each of us own a part of the show?* | 16 | th-g5-imagine; th-g5-show | TH:Cr1.1.5a; TH:Cr1.1.5c; TH:Cr2.1.5a; TH:Cr2.1.5b; TH:Cr3.1.5a; TH:Pr6.1.5a | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3); performing (Pr6). Works: Hans Christian Andersen's The Steadfast Tin Soldier (Denmark, 1838, public domain), retold by Curio Class: a character who cannot speak, so his body and his inner thoughts carry the story; William Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream (England, about 1595, public domain), Bottom's waking speech in Act 4, a character thinking aloud, cut by Curio Class to a few lines | Retrieval in day 2's opener: the given-circumstances card (th-g4-imagine), named jobs and the critique-protocol (th-g4-show). TH:Cr1.1.5a: name physical traits that could show what a character is like inside (the soldier stands straight on one leg). TH:Cr1.1.5c: picture how what a character thinks inside shapes the story and its circumstances, in a few written lines of inner thought. TH:Cr2.1.5a: shape an original scene from the group's questions about a character and the situation they face. TH:Cr2.1.5b: take on a set job needed to share the scene casually with viewers. TH:Cr3.1.5a: revise the scene by running it and reviewing it alone (the self-review line on the rehearsal-log). TH:Pr6.1.5a: share the scene in a relaxed way with viewers. Technique: the inner-thought line spoken as an aside and then hidden in the body; the self-review run. Words: inner, quality, thought, situation, original, question, job, self-review, revise, viewer, share. Showing: each group's original scene about a character with no words or few, shared relaxed with a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 142, written day 143), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.5a, a guide look-for on the showing: a physical trait that shows what the student's character is like inside; TH:Cr2.1.5b, a guide look-for on the showing: the student'