nt sign-off form.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
  which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
  cultural or community tradition name the communities and the sources the agent studies first: unit 1 (kyogen of
  Japan), unit 2 (commedia dell'arte of Italy), unit 4 (Efua Sutherland's Ghana; Taki Rua and te ao Māori), unit 5
  (Lorraine Hansberry's Chicago and the housing covenant case), unit 6 (Sanskrit drama of India), unit 7 (kabuki of
  Japan), unit 8 (Spiderwoman Theater's Kuna and Rappahannock founders; Kamiriithu of Kenya), unit 10 (Yuan drama of
  China), unit 11 (Ilbijerri's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities) and unit 12 (Woza Albert! and
  apartheid South Africa; Native Voices at the Autry). No student plays a character by an accent, a costume of
  another culture or a stereotype; no student is cast against their wish; no role play puts a student in a victim's
  or a harmer's place, and no scene stages bullying, abuse or harassment (APPROACH "Works and their settings").
  Every social problem or community issue comes from a class list of local, non-partisan public matters or an
  invented town, marked the same (values rule), and never from a student's own family, health, body or a disclosure.
  Every task that ties a character or a choice to a student's own life or beliefs (units 1, 5, 9 for tastes and
  beliefs; units 4, 8, 12 for community) offers an invented or public option marked the same (APPROACH "Sensitive
  ground"). Each section carries health's steps for "If a student tells you something"
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
- **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: two 40-minute sessions a week, a clear floor, step
  cards, adapted scenes, research and object cards, found and low-cost materials. Activities: the actor's tools,
  staging, design and devising taught in steps; plays, forms and companies studied with their setting; rehearsal with
  a critique and a revision; a showing each unit. Outputs: a portfolio folder of 12 pieces over the band with
  reflections, 12 showings, two marks a unit per student on the main look-for. Outcome claimed: met marks on the
  year's 24 ids and growth on the look-fors. Nothing here has been tried with students, and Curio Class makes no claim
  that arts study raises any other result (APPROACH "Sources").

## Seams

### Picked up from Theater, grades 3 to 5

Every grade 6 row assumes the grade 5 row in its thread (data/progressions/arts.csv, `assumes` and `hands_on` cells
answer at both ends), last used one grade back, so each opens as a retrieval task inside day 2 of its unit with its
ten-minute reteach for a student new to theater. Grade 5 units and days are as Theater, grades 3 to 5 states them
(band course days of that frame; its year 5 runs days 129 to 192), and its handoff table names these same grade 6 rows.

| Thread | Grade 5 row hands on (unit, days there) | Grade 6 row that picks it up (unit, course days) | What changes in grade 6 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Imagining | th-g5-imagine (unit 9, days 129 to 144): an original scene shaped from questions about a character's inner life | th-g6-imagine (unit 2, days 19 to 32) | The problem becomes staging, and ideas are analyzed in rehearsal, not only offered |
| Showing | th-g5-show (unit 9, days 129 to 144): a set job in a group piece, self-review, an informal share | th-g6-show (unit 4, days 49 to 62) | Offers are built on, choices weighed, and the piece adapted for its audience |
| Actor | th-g5-actor (unit 10, days 145 to 162): body and voice build a character; warm-ups matched to the piece | th-g6-actor (unit 1, days 1 to 18) | The character is built from given circumstances and shown with chosen body choices |
| Design | th-g5-design (unit 11, days 165 to 180): tech carries a theme; a fix invented for a rehearsal problem | th-g6-design (unit 3, days 35 to 48) | The plan is tried in rehearsal and a show's design is assessed |
| Story | th-g5-story (unit 12, days 183 to 192): motives named; versions from several cultures compared | th-g6-story (unit 2, days 19 to 32) | Key events are marked in a script, two versions of one stage story compared, the era researched |
| World | th-g5-world (unit 11, days 165 to 180): a drama ties a person to a community; a past or worldwide problem | th-g6-world (unit 4, days 49 to 62) | A shared theme or social problem is voiced, and the views behind a judgment named |
| Noticing | th-g5-notice (unit 10, days 145 to 162): a reaction backed by personal experience | th-g6-notice (unit 1, days 1 to 18) | Reactions are written down, and the student names an artist's experience and their own tastes |
| Judging | th-g5-judge (unit 12, days 183 to 192): a judging guide made and used | th-g6-judge (unit 3, days 35 to 48) | A written judgment needs evidence and criteria, and the work's audience and aim are named |

