ly or not yet. Out: several elements weighed together (grade 7), the real venue (grade 8). Tools: cue sheet, design sketch sheet and criteria card, Curio Class's own; a cue-calling practice tool or its printed cue cards. Free path: the room's light switch and blinds, voice and found-object sound, cardboard. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions  |  After unit 2 because design fixes a problem in a scene already staged and marked; judging grows from unit 1's written reaction and needs the criteria the class sets here. Before unit 4 because the devised piece uses a designed cue and the class criteria. The prop-sketch, cue-sheet, criteria-card and reason-because models start here 
 4  |  6  |  Our shared story. *How can a devised scene voice a problem many people share, and how do a community's views shape how a play is judged?*  |  14  |  th-g6-show; th-g6-world  |  TH:Cr2.1.6b; TH:Cr3.1.6a; TH:Pr6.1.6a; TH:Cn10.1.6a; TH:Cn11.1.6a; TH:Re8.1.6b  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 5's set job in a group piece (th-g5-show) and a drama's community tie (th-g5-world). TH:Cn10.1.6a: explain how what characters do and want shapes a view of a community's culture (Foriwa's townspeople, from Curio Class's description). TH:Cn11.1.6a: pick out a theme everyone shares or a common social problem and voice it through a devised scene (from a class list of local, non-partisan public matters, such as a park no one uses or a library closing early, or an invented town, marked the same). TH:Cr2.1.6b: offer ideas and take up others' while devising ("yes, and", on the rehearsal log). TH:Cr3.1.6a: explain and weigh choices to polish the piece (the critique, then a revision). TH:Pr6.1.6a: adapt the piece for its audience and show it informally. TH:Re8.1.6b: name cultural views that may shape how a drama work is judged (how the play's own town and a far audience might judge Foriwa; Taki Rua's choice to play in te reo Māori). Words: theme, social problem, offer, adapt, polish, culture. Showing: an informal showing for families or a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr2.1.6b, the kept rehearsal log: two offers the student made and one taken up from another; TH:Cr3.1.6a, the kept critique-and-revise note: a choice weighed and what changed; TH:Pr6.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: the piece shown, with the change made for this audience named on the log; TH:Cn11.1.6a, a guide look-for on the showing: the shared theme or problem clear in the scene; TH:Cn10.1.6a, a written item: one character's action in an unseen description and the view of the community it gives; TH:Re8.1.6b, a written item: one cultural view that may shape how the described piece is judged. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. The year-end check follows this unit in week 32 and draws its items from units 1 to 3. Out: many community viewpoints woven (grade 7). Tools: rehearsal log, critique-and-revise note, two-ties card and the problem list, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, the voice, the year's kept props. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions  |  Last in grade 6 because the devised piece uses the year's characters, staging fixes, a designed cue and the class criteria, and the showing ends the year; the year-end check follows in the finals week. The two-ties model is first used here; group agreements, the rehearsal log and the critique protocol, reviewed since unit 1, are owned here 
 5  |  7  |  What does the character want? *How does a character's goal change the way an actor moves and speaks, and how do artists' own lives show in their choices?*  |  18  |  th-g7-actor; th-g7-notice  |  TH:Pr4.1.7b; TH:Pr5.1.7a; TH:Cr3.1.7b; TH:Cr1.1.7c; TH:Re7.1.7a; TH:Re8.1.7a; TH:Re8.1.7c  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 6's character built from given circumstances (th-g6-actor, unit 1, days 1 to 18) and the written reaction (th-g6-notice, unit 1). TH:Pr4.1.7b: try out different goals a character might pursue (Lomov wants a wife, or wants the meadow), playing the same lines with each. TH:Pr5.1.7a: take part in varied warm-ups and methods used in rehearsal (a physical warm-up, a tongue-twister set, an objective exercise, a hot-seat interview). TH:Cr3.1.7b: build body and voice traits that fit the chosen goal. TH:Cr1.1.7c: picture and describe what the character thinks inside and wants (an inner monologue on the character map). TH:Re7.1.7a: compare the student's written reaction to an artist's decision with a classmate's. TH:Re8.1.7a: name the choices artists made out of their own lives (Hansberry's family case, Miranda's neighborhood, Chekhov the country doctor), from biography cards Curio Class writes. TH:Re8.1.7c: interpret how the student's taste and beliefs can frame a discussion of a piece. Words: goal, want, inner, warm-up, method, trait. Showing: the quarrel scene played with two goals, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.7b, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene played with two different goals, each readable; TH:Pr5.1.7a, the kept rehearsal note: two warm-ups or methods taken part in and what each did for the scene, with the teacher's rotating-list marks from the warm-up days; TH:Cr3.1.7b, a guide look-for on the showing: body and voice traits that fit the goal; TH:Cr1.1.7c, the kept character map: a six-line inner monologue of what the character thinks and wants; TH:Re7.1.7a, a written item: the student's entry on the teacher's unseen scene set beside a partner's, with one likeness and one difference; TH:Re8.1.7a, a written item: one choice in an unseen described work drawn from the artist's life, from its biography card; TH:Re8.1.7c, a written item: how the student's taste framed the pair's talk about the unseen scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tactics against an obstacle (grade 8). Tools: character map, warm-up card, method cards, audience journal, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, the voice, printed scenes. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions  |  First in grade 7 because it reopens the floor and the routines after the summer and moves the character from circumstances to a goal and an inner life, which unit 6's staging and unit 8's woven roles use. Longest unit for the same reasons as unit 1 
 6  |  7  |  The lost ring. *How might a playwright have pictured his play's first staging, and which staging of a scene tells the story better?*  |  14  |  th-g7-imagine; th-g7-story  |  TH:Cr1.1.7a; TH:Cr2.1.7a; TH:Pr4.1.7a; TH:Cn11.2.7a; TH:Cn11.2.7b  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 6's staging fixes (th-g6-imagine, unit 2, days 19 to 32) and script markup and era research (th-g6-story, unit 2). TH:Pr4.1.7a: stage the ring scene two ways and weigh which strengthens the story (RL.7.3 practiced, not owned: how setting and characters shape the plot). TH:Cr1.1.7a: look at several viewpoints and fixes for a staging problem (the ring found in a fish must be seen by the audience; the fisherman, the guards and the audience each see it differently). TH:Cr2.1.7a: examine and defend the chosen staging in a short note using analysis, background and context (the Natyashastra's stage, a Gupta court). TH:Cn11.2.7a: research and talk about how Kalidasa may have pictured the first production (a court stage, a stage manager's opening, gesture for the chariot, no painted scenery). TH:Cn11.2.7b: study objects from the play's era and region (the coin, a ring) to explain a staging or design choice. Words: viewpoint, option, author, research, object, region. Showing: the ring scene in two stagings, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing: both stagings tell the story clearly and the group names the stronger one; TH:Cr1.1.7a, the kept idea sheet: three fixes, each from a named viewpoint; TH:Cr2.1.7a, the kept defense note (five to eight sentences: claim, analysis, background); TH:Cn11.2.7a, a written item: from an unseen description of an ancient stage, two things its author may have pictured; TH:Cn11.2.7b, a written item: an unseen object card from another era and one staging or design choice it explains. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: pacing and two productions compared (grade 8). Tools: idea sheet, defense note frame, script markup, research and object cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: a card fish and ring, chairs, cloth. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions  |  After unit 5 because the stagings need characters with goals; before unit 7 because the design unit redesigns this scene's change of place. Research of the author's first production builds on grade 6's era cards 
 7  |  7  |  Stage magic. *How do set, light, sound and costume work together on an audience's feelings, and which design do we prefer, and why?*  |  14  |  th-g7-design; th-g7-judge  |  TH:Cr1.1.7b; TH:Pr5.1.7b; TH:Cr3.1.7c; TH:Re9.1.7b; TH:Re9.1.7a; TH:Re9.1.7c  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 6's design plan tried in rehearsal (th-g6-design, unit 3, days 35 to 48) and a judgment with evidence (th-g6-judge, unit 3). TH:Cr1.1.7b: explain and present fixes for a design problem (the ring scene's change from a palace to a riverbank in seconds). TH:Pr5.1.7b: choose several tech elements to use in the design (a costume change over clothes, a light shift, a sound). TH:Cr3.1.7c: weigh the planned elements together while rehearsing, and keep, cut or change each. TH:Re9.1.7b: notice how a show's set, light, sound and costumes look and feel, and why (the kabuki print, Devlin's described work). TH:Re9.1.7a: explain what the student prefers in a piece, backed by evidence and criteria. TH:Re9.1.7c: tell how a work's aim draws in the viewers it was made for (the hanamichi brings actors among the audience). Words: element, look and feel, present, weigh, prefer, viewer. Showing: the redesigned ring scene, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.7b, a guide look-for on the two-minute presentation of the fix with its kept sketch, marked live on the showing day; TH:Pr5.1.7b, the kept cue sheet: at least three elements chosen, each with its job; TH:Cr3.1.7c, the kept tech-rehearsal note: the elements weighed and one cut or changed, with why; TH:Re9.1.7b, a written item: how two elements of an unseen described production look and feel, and why; TH:Re9.1.7a, a written item: the student's preference in the teacher's unseen scene with evidence and a criterion; TH:Re9.1.7c, a written item: how the described production's aim draws in its audience. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the real venue and simple technology (grade 8). Tools: cue sheet, design sketch sheet, criteria card, Curio Class's own; a cue-calling practice tool or its printed cue cards; a school device may play one sound cue. Free path: the room's light switch and blinds, found-object sound, cloth. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions  |  After unit 6 because the design solves that scene's change of place; preference grows from grade 6's judgment and needs unit 5's compared reactions. Before unit 8 because the woven piece uses another art and a designed cue 
 8  |  7  |  Weaving our voices. *How can one piece hold many voices from a community, and how do cultural views shape how a drama is judged?*  |  14  |  th-g7-show; th-g7-world  |  TH:Cr2.1.7b; TH:Cr3.1.7a; TH:Pr6.1.7a; TH:Cn10.1.7a; TH:Cn11.1.7a; TH:Re8.1.7b  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 6's offers and polishing (th-g6-show, unit 4, days 49 to 62) and the shared theme (th-g6-world, unit 4). TH:Cn10.1.7a: weave several viewpoints and ideas from across a community into the piece (five voices from Curio Class's invented interview cards for one public place, or the class's own public-place stories). TH:Cn11.1.7a: deepen the piece's meaning and conflict with media, art, dance or music from its setting (a public domain song, an open-access image held up, a found sound). TH:Cr2.1.7b: show respect for oneself, others and each role while preparing. TH:Cr3.1.7a: rehearse with focus to study and improve choices. TH:Pr6.1.7a: take part in the rehearsals ahead of the showing. TH:Re8.1.7b: describe how cultural views can shape how a drama work is judged (Kamiriithu judged by its village and by the government). Words: viewpoint, community, conflict, setting, respect, focus. Showing: the woven piece for families or another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cn10.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing: at least three viewpoints heard in the piece; TH:Cn11.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing with the log line: one art from the setting and the conflict it deepens; TH:Cr2.1.7b, the teacher's rotating-list marks on the agreements look-for over the rehearsal days, with the student's log line on how they kept each role; TH:Cr3.1.7a, the kept critique-and-revise note: one choice studied in rehearsal and how it improved; TH:Pr6.1.7a, the kept rehearsal log: each rehearsal with the student's role and one thing done (a missed rehearsal has a make-up task); TH:Re8.1.7b, a written item: an unseen description of a play judged two ways by two audiences, and how