 9  |  8  |  Tactics. *How does a character get past an obstacle, and how do we judge an actor's choices by criteria and not by taste alone?*  |  18  |  th-g8-actor; th-g8-notice  |  TH:Pr4.1.8b; TH:Pr5.1.8a; TH:Cr3.1.8b; TH:Cr1.1.8c; TH:Re7.1.8a; TH:Re8.1.8a; TH:Re8.1.8c  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 7's goals and inner thoughts (th-g7-actor, unit 5, days 65 to 82) and compared reactions (th-g7-notice, unit 5). TH:Cr1.1.8c: build a character by describing inner thoughts, goals and motives (a motive line on the character map). TH:Pr4.1.8b: use varied goals and tactics to get past an obstacle (Jack wants consent; Lady Bracknell is the obstacle; charm, facts, flattery, retreat), switching tactics at a beat. TH:Pr5.1.8a: apply many acting techniques to grow skill in rehearsal (a Stanislavski objective, a Spolin game, a status exercise). TH:Cr3.1.8b: polish body, voice and bodily-response traits (how the body takes news: a breath, a freeze, a turn). TH:Re7.1.8a: judge the decisions artists made in a piece using criteria (the class criteria card, as review). TH:Re8.1.8a: notice and talk about artists' decisions while joining in or watching. TH:Re8.1.8c: use the student's taste and beliefs to judge a drama work, beside the criteria. SL.8.4 practiced, not owned. Words: tactic, obstacle, motive, technique, polish, bodily response. Showing: the interview scene, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.8c, the kept character map: inner thoughts, a goal and a motive; TH:Pr4.1.8b, a guide look-for on the showing: three tactics, each switched at a beat; TH:Pr5.1.8a, the kept technique note: two techniques applied and what each changed in rehearsal; TH:Cr3.1.8b, a guide look-for on the showing: a bodily response to the key line, held; TH:Re7.1.8a, a written item: one decision in the teacher's unseen scene judged against two criteria; TH:Re8.1.8a, a written item: the decision the pair noticed and talked about while watching, with what each said; TH:Re8.1.8c, a written item: the student's taste or belief named beside the criteria judgment, and how the two differ. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a script's given circumstances tested for believability (Level 1). Tools: character map, technique cards, criteria card, audience journal, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, the voice, printed scenes. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions  |  First in grade 8 because it reopens the floor and routines and moves the character to tactics and motives, which unit 10's pacing and unit 12's polished piece use; judging with criteria begins here and unit 11 pairs it with taste and effect. Longest unit for the same reasons as unit 1 
 10  |  8  |  The chalk circle. *How does one story change from one production to another, and how can research into an era build an original drama idea?*  |  14  |  th-g8-imagine; th-g8-story  |  TH:Cr1.1.8a; TH:Cr2.1.8a; TH:Pr4.1.8a; TH:Cn11.2.8a; TH:Cn11.2.8b  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 7's viewpoints and defended staging (th-g7-imagine, unit 6, days 83 to 96) and the author's first production and period objects (th-g7-story, unit 6). TH:Pr4.1.8a: try different pacing for the test scene (slow, fast, a held pause) to find the one that tells it best. TH:Cn11.2.8a: research the Yuan play's story parts and compare them with the later production's (who keeps the child, and why). TH:Cn11.2.8b: find and use objects from an era and place to shape staging and design (the troupe painting's costumes, an object card). TH:Cr1.1.8a: imagine and explore several viewpoints and fixes for a staging problem (the test must be clear and safe: a cloth circle, a doll or bundle, two claimants). TH:Cr2.1.8a: draw on research and cultural and historical background to develop an original drama idea (a group's own short scene about a fair test, set in a researched era and place from the card set). Words: pacing, production, compare, era, background, original. Showing: the test scene and the groups' original scenes, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.8a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene in two paces, the chosen one named; TH:Cn11.2.8a, a written item: an unseen description of two productions of one play, two story parts compared; TH:Cn11.2.8b, the kept staging note: one object card and the staging or design choice it shaped; TH:Cr1.1.8a, the kept idea sheet: three viewpoints, each with its fix; TH:Cr2.1.8a, the kept idea sheet for the original scene: the researched background from two cards and the idea built on it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a production concept built from research (Level 1). Tools: idea sheet, script markup, story map, research and object cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: a cloth or chalk circle, a rolled-cloth bundle, chairs. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions  |  After unit 9 because pacing a scene needs characters with tactics; before unit 11 because the venue design is made for these original scenes. Comparing productions grows from grade 7's author-and-object research 
 11  |  8  |  The room is the stage. *How does the place where a show happens shape its design, and how do we judge a show's effect on its audience?*  |  14  |  th-g8-design; th-g8-judge  |  TH:Cr1.1.8b; TH:Pr5.1.8b; TH:Cr3.1.8c; TH:Re9.1.8b; TH:Re9.1.8a; TH:Re9.1.8c  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 7's elements weighed (th-g7-design, unit 7, days 99 to 112) and preference with evidence (th-g7-judge, unit 7). TH:Cr1.1.8b: imagine and explore fixes for design problems in the real place the class will perform (the room, hall or yard: sight lines, entrances, noise, light). TH:Pr5.1.8b: combine several tech elements into one design. TH:Cr3.1.8c: use simple technology (LED flashlights with colored film, the room's switch, a found-object sound table, or a school device playing a sound cue) to carry out and refine the tech plan in rehearsal. TH:Re9.1.8b: judge a show's aesthetic choices through its production elements. TH:Re9.1.8a: react to a play by pairing evidence, personal taste and artistic criteria (W.8.1 practiced, not owned). TH:Re9.1.8c: judge a work's effect on its intended audience. Words: venue, combine, refine, technology, effect, intended audience. Showing: unit 10's original scenes designed for the real space, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.8b, the kept venue plan: a floor plan of the real space with two problems and their fixes; TH:Pr5.1.8b, the kept cue sheet: three elements combined, each cue with its job; TH:Cr3.1.8c, a guide look-for on the showing: the tech plan run with simple technology, with the cue refined after the tech rehearsal named on the cue sheet; TH:Re9.1.8b, a written item: an unseen described production's aesthetic choice judged through two production elements; TH:Re9.1.8a, a written item: a paragraph on the teacher's unseen scene pairing evidence, taste and a criterion; TH:Re9.1.8c, a written item: the described production's effect on its intended audience, with a reason. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: research and technology shaping a design folio (Level 1). Tools: venue plan sheet, cue sheet, criteria card, Curio Class's own; a cue-calling practice tool or its printed cue cards. Free path: the room's switch and blinds, found-object sound, card. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions  |  After unit 10 because the design serves those original scenes; the paired judgment grows from unit 9's criteria and grade 7's preference. Before unit 12 because the polished staging uses this venue plan and cue sheet 
 12  |  8  |  Theater for a change. *How can different kinds of drama look at an issue facing our community from several viewpoints, and how do cultural views shape how such work is judged?*  |  14  |  th-g8-show; th-g8-world  |  TH:Cr2.1.8b; TH:Cr3.1.8a; TH:Pr6.1.8a; TH:Cn10.1.8a; TH:Cn11.1.8a; TH:Re8.1.8b  |  Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 7's respect, focus and rehearsals (th-g7-show, unit 8, days 113 to 126) and many viewpoints with another art (th-g7-world, unit 8). TH:Cn10.1.8a: look at an issue facing the community from several viewpoints through drama (a class-chosen local, non-partisan public issue, such as a crosswalk or a closed pool, or an invented town, marked the same). TH:Cn11.1.8a: try varied kinds of drama to examine the issue (a forum scene, a two-actor many-roles scene, a short documentary-style scene from invented interview cards). TH:Cr2.1.8b: take turns leading and sharing jobs to set group goals (rotating director, stage manager and designer). TH:Cr3.1.8a: rework the piece by running it and analyzing the results. TH:Pr6.1.8a: stage the polished, rehearsed piece for an audience. TH:Re8.1.8b: analyze how cultural views shape how a work is judged (Woza Albert! judged in Johannesburg and abroad; a forum scene judged by its own audience). Words: issue, viewpoint, kind, lead, rework, society. Showing: the polished piece for families or another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cn10.1.8a, a guide look-for on the showing: at least three viewpoints on the issue; TH:Cn11.1.8a, the kept rehearsal log: two kinds of drama tried on the issue, one kept and why; TH:Cr2.1.8b, the kept log: the student's turn as lead with the goal set, and a shared job; TH:Cr3.1.8a, the kept critique-and-revise note: a run, its analysis and the change; TH:Pr6.1.8a, a guide look-for on the showing: a polished piece staged (cues on time, entrances clean, focus held); TH:Re8.1.8b, a written item: an unseen description of a work judged by two audiences, analyzing how cultural views shape each judgment. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. The year-end check follows this unit in week 32 and draws its items from units 9 to 11. Out: a script staged for an audience the group chooses (Level 1). Tools: rehearsal log, interview cards, critique-and-revise note, the year's cue sheets, Curio Class's own. Free path: the voice, chairs, the year's kept props. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions  |  Last in the band because the polished staging uses the year's tactics, original scenes and venue design, and the showing ends the band; the year-end check follows in the finals week, and the threads go on to Level 1 


