tions and simple and compound sentences; the fall oral reading screen is diagnostic (warm-ups). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.2a (unit 3); L.3.2b (unit 4); L.3.2c, L.3.5b, L.3.5c (unit 2). | The base text's module 1, kept whole: it opens the year with close reading, the discussion routine, independent reading and the exit ticket, which every later unit uses, and it already teaches and assesses word meaning in the stories (RL.3.4, L.3.4), the recorded reading (RF.3.4, SL.3.5) and the revised reading contract (W.3.5), so those rows sit here. Short whole texts come before the long chapter book of unit 3. Two days are added for the start-of-year screens (long words, oral reading, a writing sample) | Candidate: rubric 1g to 1n (the long-word sequence taught in order, with checks and next steps), 1t and 1u (fluency practice with checks: the recorded reading), 2i (the discussion routine written with teacher guidance), 2e (a text set whose children and places vary) | Children kept from school by poverty, distance or work: framed through their effort, not the danger; no war or violence shown. The Booker T. Washington lines touch slavery: named plainly, people named as enslaved people, the excerpt short and previewed by the teacher, with its own short context (social studies teaches slavery later, in its unit 5; the task asks about the selection, not prior history). No student is asked to share family reading struggles or literacy. Each place is real and named, never a generic "poor country". Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |
