 students' drafts: regular and irregular verbs (L.3.1d), simple past, present and future tense (1e), comparative and superlative adjectives and adverbs (1g, "the smallest frog", "jumps farther"), coordinating and subordinating conjunctions (L.3.1h) and simple, compound and complex sentences combined from the frog texts (L.3.1i); plurals (1b) and abstract nouns (1c) are continued in unit 4, the function of parts of speech (1a) and agreement (1f) in unit 3. Review and extension: e3-literal-nonliteral on the poems and tales, now with real-life connections and shades of meaning for knowing and wondering (L.3.5b and c); e3-fluency on poems read aloud and a poem recording (RF.3.4b, SL.3.5); e3-word-strategies on the frog words; e3-main-idea and e3-central-message (myths here) on the tales and articles. Two frog texts read side by side without the comparison task (grade 2's comparison, in review; RI.3.9 is taught in unit 4). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.1a, L.3.1f (unit 3); L.3.3b (unit 3, unit 4). | The base text's module 2: research needs unit 1's routines and main idea, and the frog book is the year's first piece typed and published. The base text teaches and assesses verbs, tenses, comparatives and complex sentences on the frog writing, so e3-word-forms and e3-sentences sit here. Science leans same year (life cycles, traits) | Candidate: rubric 2s and 2t (research taught, then used), 2q (planning, revising and publishing with technology), 2m and 2n (sentence work from the anchor, then in the student's own editing) | Animals eating and being eaten described plainly, not graphically. Web research on a teacher-chosen list only; no student account. Myths credited to the people who tell them. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-3/frame.md:159:| 3 | A chapter-book series. "Would you recommend this series to a friend, and why?" | 39 | e3-story-parts; e3-point-of-view; e3-same-author; e3-opinion; e3-speaking-reports | RL.3.5; RL.3.6; RI.3.6; RL.3.9; W.3.1; SL.3.4; SL.3.6; L.3.3b | Not used (ELA) | Anchors: book 1 of an original Curio Class chapter-book series set today (about 10,000 to 20,000 words, measure target about 620 to 820), read whole as a class; book 2 of the same series, about the same characters, read in part (at least three connected chapters, enough to compare theme, setting and plot) or as the independent book; a two-scene play script from book 1; a short informational anchor on how series books are made (g3-u3-04). Chapter, scene and stanza used when talking and writing, and how each part builds on the ones before (RL.3.5): what each chapter adds to the ones before, followed on the narrative arc; what the second scene of the script adds to the first; and, returning to one unit 2 poem of two or more stanzas, what the later stanza adds to the earlier. The reader's own view told apart from the narrator's and characters' (RL.3.6) and from an author's in a short article (RI.3.6); the two books compared on theme, setting and plot (RL.3.9). Writing: a book review recommending the series or not, with reasons in order, linking words and a conclusion (W.3.1a to d), taken through the process, with titles capitalized (L.3.2a); a scene from book 1 rewritten (review of e3-narrative). Speaking: the revised scene presented to the class as a report on the book with facts and descriptive details, at a pace listeners can follow (SL.3.4), in complete sentences where the task asks for detail (SL.3.6), read fluently (review of e3-fluency); the scene's spoken delivery set beside its written script, the conventions each uses named (sentences a listener can follow, a speaker's pauses and stress, against the script's punctuation, paragraphs and stage directions), then both rehearsed and edited to fit their own form, on a short paired model and a check where each student names and applies one difference (L.3.3b). Grammar continued from unit 2 on the books' sentences: what each part of speech does in a sentence (L.3.1a) and subject-verb and pronoun agreement (L.3.1f). A text-based discussion of the class's opinions. Review: e3-character-actions and e3-literal-nonliteral on the book. The unit's word list, studied and spelled. | The base text's module 3, with its classic replaced by an original two-book series (rule 29; the progression stitch, item 6). It needs unit 1's character work and unit 2's narrative arc and figurative language; the book review is the year's first opinion piece, built on unit 2's reasons-and-links work. The base text's performance task here is the presented scene (SL.3.4, SL.3.6) and its book-review revision assesses L.3.3b, so e3-speaking-reports sits here. One long text read whole | Candidate: rubric 2a (one long text read whole, with a reason for its place), 2j (a speaking task with look-fors: the presented scene) | The series is written to the grade 3 text spec; a family or school problem in it is handled gently. The optional classic for independent reading is The Wonderful Wizard of Oz (1900), not Peter Pan, whose "tribe" chapters stereotype Native people. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-4/frame.md:152:## Units
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-4/frame.md:156:| 1 | Poems and becoming a writer. "How does reading poems help someone become a writer?" | 38 | e4-evidence-inference; e4-theme-summary; e4-character-depth; e4-lit-word-meaning; e4-poetry-drama; e4-narration-pov; e4-reading-range; e4-long-words; e4-fluency; e4-conventions; e4-writing-tasks; e4-discussion; e4-presenting; e4-informative; e4-writing-process; e4-grammar | RL.4.1; RI.4.1; W.4.9; RL.4.2; RL.4.3; RL.4.4; L.4.5; RL.4.5; RL.4.7; RL.4.6; RL.4.10; RI.4.10; RF.4.3; RF.4.4; L.4.2; W.4.4; W.4.10; SL.4.1; SL.4.4; SL.4.5; W.4.2; W.4.5; W.4.6; L.4.1 | Not used (ELA) | Anchors: an original Curio Class verse novel read whole (a present-day boy who thinks poems are not for him writes a year of poem-journal entries for his teacher; about 8,000 to 12,000 words, measure below the band, meaning above it); public-domain poems the boy reads (Robert Frost and William Carlos Williams from 1923, Langston Hughes from his 1926 book, Emily Dickinson, Christina Rossetti, leaving out her "Who Has Seen the Wind?", which grade 6 reads), each with a short original poet biography (measure target about 740 to 950); one short prose story told beside a verse retelling of the same moment, and a short drama scene from the same story. Referring to details when explaining what a text says and when inferring, in talk and in writing on literary texts (RL.4.1, RI.4.1, W.4.9a; 9b, informational texts, is continued in unit 2); a theme of a poem or of the verse novel from its details, and a summary (RL.4.2); the boy's change across the year described in depth (RL.4.3); similes and metaphors explained in context, idioms and proverbs, words linked to antonyms and near-synonyms (RL.4.4, L.4.5a to c; allusions to myth are taught in unit 3 with the myths); what makes a poem a poem (verse, rhythm, meter, line), drama's parts on the short scene, and how prose differs (RL.4.5); the prose story set beside a recorded reading of it and the drama scene beside a staged reading, students naming where the presentation follows particular descriptions or directions in the text (RL.4.7; the poem recordings are practice, the unit 3 play review); first-person narration of the verse novel against third-person poems and the prose story (RL.4.6). Independent reading launched in the 4 to 5 band (RL.4.10, RI.4.10). Writing: daily exit tickets and a four-paragraph informative essay on what inspires a poet, with introduction, body paragraphs grouped by aspect, facts, details and quotations from the biographies and poems, linking words, domain words and a conclusion (W.4.2a to e; the essay-structure model introduced), quotations punctuated (L.4.2b), with the rhetorical-situation model introduced (who reads it, why, what form) (W.4.4, W.4.10); the essay planned, critiqued against look-fors, revised and edited with peer and teacher feedback (W.4.5; using technology to produce and publish, W.4.6, is continued in unit 2 with one typed page and an online exchange); fragments and run-ons found and fixed in the essay (L.4.1f; modals L.4.1c and adjective order L.4.1d are continued in unit 2, progressive tenses 1b, prepositional phrases 1e and confused words 1g in unit 3, relative pronouns and adverbs 1a in unit 4); an original poem. Talk: discussion with roles (SL.4.1a to d); a poem presentation, the student's own poem read aloud and its inspiration explained, organized around a theme with a visual (SL.4.4, SL.4.5). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.4.3a); fluency on poems (RF.4.4a to c); spelling on the same patterns with reference checks (L.4.2d) and capitals (L.4.2a); the compound-sentence comma (2c) comes in unit 3. Diagnostic retrieval: the fall oral reading screen, grade 3 point of view, the discussion routine and sentences (simple, compound, complex). The unit's word list Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.4.1e, L.4.1g, L.4.2c (unit 3); W.4.9b (unit 2, unit 3). | The base text's module 1: short whole poems are the right first texts for close reading and fluency, the verse novel is a long text read whole at the start of the year, and the rhetorical-situation model, the discussion roles and the daily writing every later unit uses start here. The base text teaches and assesses the four-paragraph informative essay, its revision, fragments and run-ons and quotation punctuation in its unit 2 (W.4.2, W.4.5, L.4.1f, L.4.2b), so e4-informative, e4-writing-process and e4-grammar sit here. One day added for the start-of-year screens | Candidate: rubric 2a (a long text read whole), 1t and 1u (fluency practice with checks), 2e (poets of different eras and backgrounds), 2q (planning, critique and revision taught) | The verse novel touches the death of a pet (gentle, previewed). Students' own poems are shared by choice; no student is asked to write about family loss. Poets' biographies note hard lives plainly without dwelling. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-4/frame.md:157:| 2 | Animal defenses. "How do animals' bodies and behaviors help them survive?" | 39 | e4-main-idea-summary; e4-explain-events; e4-text-structure; e4-visual-info; e4-integrate-two; e4-academic-words; e4-word-strategies; e4-narrative; e4-research | RI.4.2; SL.4.2; RI.4.3; RI.4.5; RI.4.7; RI.4.9; RI.4.4; L.4.6; L.4.4; W.4.3; W.4.7; W.4.8 | Not used (ELA). Science grade 4 builds on it later (structures and functions of animals, its unit 6) | Anchors: an original Curio Class informational book on animal defenses (armor, poison, camouflage, mimicry; sections of 600 to 1,000 words with diagrams, measure target about 740 to 870, the lower half of the band); federal wildlife pages on a few defenses (public domain); expert-group texts on one animal each (a pufferfish, an armadillo, a skunk, a porcupine, an octopus); an original choose-your-own-adventure story as the narrative model. Main idea, how key details support it, and a written summary (RI.4.2); paraphrasing a short video or talk (SL.4.2); how a defense works, step by step and why (RI.4.3); chronology, comparison, cause and effect, problem and solution, sorted by structure (RI.4.5); a labeled diagram and a simple graph explained against the text (RI.4.7); two texts on one animal joined in writing (RI.4.9); the unit's words learned and used, with precise action words (RI.4.4, L.4.6), context clues of definition, example and restatement, Greek and Latin roots, dictionary and thesaurus (L.4.4a to c). Research in a notebook on the student's expert animal: aspects investigated, notes sorted into categories the student makes, a list of sources (W.4.7, W.4.8; the citation model introduced), drawing evidence from the informational texts (W.4.9b, continued from unit 1). Writing: an informative essay on how the animal's defenses help it survive, now with sections under headings, facts and quotations, linking words, domain words and a conclusion (W.4.2 used again on the essay-structure model from unit 1); a choose-your-own-adventure narrative about the animal, oriented, with dialogue punctuated (L.4.2b), description, transitions, sensory words and endings that follow (W.4.3a to e); both planned, critiqued, revised and edited (W.4.5 used again), and using a teacher-managed internet route with no student account, partners exchange a draft or comments, revise from the response and publish the finished writing, each student typing at least one page in one sitting (W.4.6, continued from unit 1). Grammar taught on the animal texts and drafts: modal auxiliaries (L.4.1c, "can", "may", "must") and adjective order (L.4.1d, "a small spiny fish"). Review: e4-evidence-inference, e4-discussion. The unit's word list | The base text's module 2: the year's research needs unit 1's evidence, essay and discussion routines; the narrative builds on facts from the research. The animal texts teach the structure-function content ELA needs; science models it later in the year. Every informational structure the history set in unit 3 uses is taught here first | Candidate: rubric 2s and 2t (research taught then used), 2q (publishing with technology and an online exchange), 2l (an informative piece with sources) | Predators and prey described plainly, not graphically. Web sources from a teacher-chosen list; the draft exchange runs through a teacher-managed route; no student account. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-4/frame.md:158:| 3 | The Erie Canal debate, New York 1808 to 1825. "Was the canal worth what it cost, and to whom?" | 39 | e4-reasons-evidence; e4-traditional-tales; e4-opinion | RI.4.8; SL.4.3; RL.4.9; W.4.1 | Not used (ELA). Social studies grade 4 unit 5 (days 80 to 103) takes this canal set as its case of a canal, at the same time | Anchors: an original Curio Class history book on the canal debate, from the 1808 survey to the 1825 opening (measure target about 770 to 900, the lower half of the band), naming plainly that the route ran through the lands of the Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca Nations of the Haudenosaunee, and that New York's purchases of Oneida and other Haudenosaunee land in the 1780s and 1790s lacked the federal approval the law required, as the Supreme Court later held (County of Oneida v. Oneida Indian Nation, 1985; the writer checks this at source); the 1816 public memorial urging the state to build the canal (public domain, short excerpts adapted with a glossary) and a short original speech of a lawmaker of the time against the cost ("Clinton's Ditch"); short excerpts of travelers' letters and journals (public domain); an original short play about a farm family in the canal's path, read aloud and performed; a timeline 1808 to 1825; a myth set from three cultures with a quest or trials pattern (the labors of Heracles, Greek, including the stables cleaned by turning two rivers; an Anansi tale, Akan; Momotaro, Japanese), 500 to 1,500 words each, each credited to its people, with its own short geographic and cultural context (where the people live, who tells the tale). The unit opens with a canal launch, a gist read and a close read of the history book's overview. How the memorial's authors and the speech use reasons and evidence for each point, close read over three lessons (RI.4.8), and the reasons and evidence a speaker gives in a recorded talk (SL.4.3), on the claim-evidence-reasoning model with point, reason and evidence as three parts; one research day on the canal's supporters and opponents; the myths' quests and trials compared across three cultures on the narrative arc (RL.4.9), and the words they gave English (Herculean, a quest; the allusion part of RL.4.4, e4-lit-word-meaning, unit 1). The myths sit here because each is a journey against great labor, read beside the canal's own labor. The play, read aloud and then performed, compared with its script (review of e4-poetry-drama, RL.4.7). Writing: a piece urging the people of 1817 to support or oppose the canal, reasons each backed by facts and details from the texts, grouped, linked (for instance, in order to, in addition) and concluded (W.4.1a to d), drawing evidence from the history texts (W.4.9b, used again), through the process with the compound-sentence comma taught in editing (L.4.2c); a short first-person scene of a person of the canal's path written in one sitting (review of e4-narrative); a two-sided discussion where both sides are defensible. Grammar continued from unit 2 on the canal texts: progressive tenses (L.4.1b, "the crews were digging"), prepositional phrases (L.4.1e, "along the route") and confused words (L.4.1g, to, too, two; there, their). Review: e4-text-structure (the timeline and cause and effect), e4-visual-info (the timeline and a map of the route), e4-integrate-two | The base text's module 3 design (a history set, a play of a family divided, a piece urging a side, a structured discussion) moved off the American Revolution to an event grade 4 social studies teaches (the progression stitch). It needs unit 2's structures and integration, and the opinion piece needs the reasons-and-evidence work it opens. Three days inside the unit become myth days (the source map names which); the unit stays 39 days | Candidate: rubric 2e (texts from several cultures, each credited), 2v (an end task joining reading, writing and speaking), 2b (texts of the period with a reason for each) | Haudenosaunee lands taken for the canal: named plainly, the Nations named, the unlawful purchases named, never framed as empty land; the sensitivity agent checks the history and any Haudenosaunee voice against the Nations' own published accounts (brief). The laborers' danger and illness named without graphic detail. Students argue a side in writing and discussion as themselves looking back, never in role as a person of the time; no simulation. Myths credited to the people who tell them. Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-5/frame.md:168:## Units
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-5/frame.md:172:| 1 | Starting over: stories of human rights. "What does a person need to start over, and whose job is it to protect that?" | 41 | e5-quote-accurately; e5-theme-response; e5-compare-characters; e5-figurative; e5-whole-structure; e5-reading-range; e5-several-main-ideas; e5-academic-words; e5-word-strategies; e5-long-words; e5-fluency; e5-informative; e5-narrative; e5-writing-tasks; e5-writing-process; e5-discussion; e5-grammar; e5-conventions | RL.5.1; RI.5.1; W.5.9; RL.5.2; RL.5.3; RL.5.4; L.5.5; RL.5.5; RL.5.10; RI.5.10; RI.5.2; SL.5.2; RI.5.4; L.5.6; L.5.4; RF.5.3; RF.5.4; W.5.2; W.5.3; W.5.4; W.5.10; W.5.5; W.5.6; SL.5.1; L.5.1; L.5.3; L.5.2 | Not used (ELA) | Anchors: an original Curio Class novel read whole (a present-day girl whose family loses its home after her father's death and starts over doing farm work in another state; chapters named for the crops they harvest; about 30,000 to 40,000 words, measure target about 750 to 950); articles of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in the official 1948 text, each with Curio Class's own short plain explainer (measure target about 850 to 1010); a short original two-scene play from the novel's world, the model for the monologue, and a fresh short scene read alone at the end of the unit. Quoting accurately when explaining and inferring, in talk and in writing (RL.5.1, RI.5.1, W.5.9a on the novel and play, W.5.9b on the declaration articles); a theme from how the girl responds to each challenge, and a summary (RL.5.2); two characters' responses to one event compared (RL.5.3); metaphors and similes interpreted in a passage, idioms and proverbs, synonyms, antonyms and homographs (RL.5.4, L.5.5a to c); how the chapters fit the novel's arc and the scenes fit the play (RL.5.5); stories, the play and poems read in the band, the fresh scene read alone (RL.5.10); two or more main ideas of a declaration article and an explainer, with a summary, and a summary of a short talk that shows a simple chart (numbers within grade 4 math), told from the words and the chart together (RI.5.2, SL.5.2); the unit's words, with contrast and addition words (however, although, moreover) (RI.5.4, L.5.6), context of cause and comparison, Greek and Latin roots, references for pronunciation and meaning (L.5.4a to c). Writing: daily exit tickets quoting the text (W.5.10) and a literary essay on how a character's response to a challenge shows a theme, connected to one declaration article: a general observation and focus, logical grouping, quotations, linking words (in contrast, especially), precise words, a conclusion (W.5.2a to e, W.5.4); a monologue in the voice of a character of the novel, oriented, with description, pacing, sensory detail and a conclusion (W.5.3a to e), built from short notes on the novel's setting and the farm work (W.5.8 previewed in its grade 4 form; taught in unit 2), planned, critiqued, rewritten where a new approach works better and edited (W.5.5), then performed and recorded as audio on a classroom device; the literary essay's revision typed (W.5.6 begins; two pages in a sitting and the shared-document exchange are continued in unit 2). Talk: discussions prepared, with roles, elaborating on others and drawing conclusions (SL.5.1a to d). Grammar from the novel's sentences: conjunctions, prepositions, interjections (L.5.1a), perfect tenses (L.5.1b), tense for time and sequence (L.5.1c), tense shifts (L.5.1d), correlative conjunctions (L.5.1e), expanding, combining and reducing sentences (L.5.3a; 3b, varieties of English, is taught in unit 2 with the narrators). Conventions in editing: series commas, introductory commas, yes and no, tag questions ("It's hard, isn't it?"), direct address, titles, spelling (L.5.2a to e). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.5.3a); fluency on the novel's passages and the monologue rehearsed for its recording (RF.5.4a to c). Retrieval: grade 4 fragments and run-ons and discussion roles; the fall oral reading check is a diagnostic. The unit's word list. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.5.3b (unit 2, unit 4). | The base text's module 1: a long novel read whole at the start of the year builds the close reading, quoting, discussion and essay routines every later unit uses, and the grammar and conventions thread starts on its sentences. The base text teaches and assesses the monologue here (W.5.3, its performance on RF.5.4, W.5.4, W.5.5), so e5-narrative and e5-writing-process sit here. Its length (41 days, the year's longest) fits the 18 rows it opens | Candidate: rubric 2a (a long text read whole), 1g to 1n (the long-word sequence with checks), 2i (discussion routines taught with guidance), 1t and 1u (fluency practice with checks: the recorded monologue) | A father's death, a family's loss of its home, hard farm work and moving for work: previewed, told with dignity, never as pity. No student is asked about family housing, money, work or immigration status; the monologue speaks as a character of the novel, never as the student. The declaration is presented as what people agreed all should have; no current political dispute is argued. Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-5/frame.md:173:| 2 | The rainforest. "How do the living things of El Yunque depend on each other, and how do people protect it?" | 39 | e5-relationships; e5-compare-structures; e5-locate-sources; e5-research; e5-narrator-influence; e5-genre-compare | RI.5.3; RI.5.5; RI.5.7; W.5.7; W.5.8; RL.5.6; L.5.3b; RL.5.9 | Not used (ELA). Leans on science grade 5 (matter and energy in ecosystems) | Anchors: an original Curio Class account of a canopy scientist's year in El Yunque National Forest in Puerto Rico (measure target about 850 to 1010); federal forest service pages on El Yunque, its coquí frogs, parrots and hurricanes (public domain); an original problem and solution article on protecting the forest; the base text's own CC BY texts on rainforest structure, adapted; two original Curio Class rainforest adventure stories in one genre (2,000 to 4,000 words each, measure target about 850 to 1010), one told in the first person by a girl lost for a day in El Yunque, one in the third person about a boy following a parrot survey, on the same theme of learning to read the forest. How plants, animals and people in the forest interact, from specific information (RI.5.3); the scientist's chronology compared with the problem and solution article (RI.5.5); answers found quickly across several print and digital sources, teacher-chosen first, then a supervised search (RI.5.7). How each narrator's point of view shapes how the same kind of event is described (RL.5.6); the two adventure stories compared on how they approach the theme (RL.5.9); a literary essay on how one story's sensory language builds its setting, quoting the text (W.5.2 and W.5.9 used again). Research on one way to protect the forest from several sources, summarized or paraphrased in notes, with a list of sources (W.5.7, W.5.8). Writing: a first-person narrative of a day in the canopy as a researcher or animal of the forest, with dialogue, pacing, transitional clauses and sensory detail (W.5.3 used again); planned, critiqued, rewritten and edited, typed at least two pages in a sitting, and partners exchange drafts and respond through a shared document on a teacher-managed route with no student account before publishing on a teacher-managed class page (W.5.6, continued from unit 1). Varieties of English named in the stories' dialogue and narration (L.5.3b; compared and contrasted in unit 4). Review: e5-several-main-ideas, e5-academic-words (biodiversity, canopy, species), e5-quote-accurately, e5-conventions in editing. The unit's word list | The base text's module 2, on a US rainforest with federal sources the class can read freely. Its second unit is literary (two stories, the narrator's point of view, stories in one genre compared, a literary essay), so e5-narrator-influence and e5-genre-compare sit here. It needs unit 1's quoting, main ideas and process routine; the research and narrative here build the source-tracking and the two-page typed piece units 3 and 4 use | Candidate: rubric 2s and 2t (research taught, then used), 2q (rewriting, publishing and exchanging drafts with technology) | Hurricanes named plainly (2017's Hurricane Maria damaged the forest); some students or families may have lived through it, so a teacher note and a different reading are offered. Puerto Rico's people named as US citizens with their own history, not as visitors' scenery; the sensitivity agent checks the original texts against Puerto Rican sources (brief). A child lost in a forest stays safe and is found within the day. Open searching supervised; no student account. Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-5/frame.md:174:| 3 | Athletes who led change. "What made these athletes leaders of change, beyond winning?" | 40 | e5-multiple-accounts; e5-reasons-to-points; e5-integrate-several; e5-opinion; e5-presenting | RI.5.6; RI.5.8; SL.5.3; RI.5.9; W.5.1; SL.5.4; SL.5.5; SL.5.6 | Not used (ELA). Leans on social studies grade 5 (civil rights; sourcing and corroboration) | Anchors: an original Curio Class biography of Jackie Robinson read whole (about 15,000 to 20,000 words, measure target about 850 to 1010); his 1949 testimony to a House committee (public domain), short newspaper reports of 1947 and a teammate's later memory, as several accounts (each report and the memory cleared by a copyright renewal search; any that is not clear is replaced by Curio Class's own account built from the facts it reports); short original biographies of other athletes who led change (Roberto Clemente, Wilma Rudolph, Jim Abbott); a short Curio Class explainer on segregation in the 1940s, since social studies teaches it later in the year. Several accounts of Robinson's first season analysed for the points of view they represent (RI.5.6); which reasons and evidence support which point in texts arguing what made an athlete a leader, on the argument map, introduced here (RI.5.8), and a speaker's points summarized with how each claim is supported (SL.5.3); several biographies joined to speak and write knowledgeably, tracking which idea came from which source (RI.5.9). Writing: an opinion essay on which qualities most made the athlete a leader of change, planned on the argument map, reasons in logical order, linked with clauses (consequently, specifically), and concluded (W.5.1a to d); an informative essay comparing how two athletes led change, from several biographies (W.5.2 used again, RI.5.9). Talk: a multimedia presentation on another athlete who led change, opinion and reasons sequenced logically, with a visual display, and speech adapted from the small 